            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A twin prime pair",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " is a pair of primes that differ by 2. The first three twin prime pairs are ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "(3,5),\\ (5,7)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "(11,13)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "twin prime conjecture",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " makes the claim that there are infinitely many twin prime pairs. It is thought to be true, no one has managed to prove it.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q32-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "List the first ten twin prime pairs.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(3,5),\\ (5,7),\\ (11,13),\\ (17,19),\\ (29,31),\\ (41,43),\\ (59,61),\\ (71,73),\\ (101,103),\\ (107,109)$",
          "worked": "Test candidate primes in increasing order by checking prime divisors up to their square roots. Keep pairs whose difference is $2$. The first ten are\n\n$(3,5),\\ (5,7),\\ (11,13),\\ (17,19),\\ (29,31),\\ (41,43),\\ (59,61),\\ (71,73),\\ (101,103),\\ (107,109)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q32-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that there is only one value of $n$ for which $n$, $n+2$ and $n+4$ are all prime.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n=3$, giving $3,5,7$.\n\nOne of the three numbers is divisible by $3$, so it must equal $3$.",
          "worked": "The numbers $n$, $n+2$ and $n+4$ have three different remainders when divided by $3$, so exactly one is divisible by $3$. Since all three are prime, that number must be $3$.\n\nIf $n+2=3$, then $n=1$, which is not prime. If $n+4=3$, then $n=-1$, which is not prime. Therefore $n=3$ is the only possibility.\n\nFor $n=3$, the three numbers are $3,5,7$, all prime. Hence the unique value is $n=3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 52
      }
    ]
  },
